<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cesena</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cesena is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on the Savio river near the Adriatic. Ruled at times by the Ordelaffi and the Malatesta, it was the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a>'s government as Duke of Romagna in 1501–1503. It is known for the Malatestiana Library, founded by Domenico Malatesta Novello in the mid-15th century, one of the first public libraries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cesena.jpg" alt="Cesena" width="320" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uomodis08 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_di_Cesena_e_le_sue_colline.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Cesena was originally an Umbrian or Etruscan town, later known as Caesena. After a brief spell under Gaulish rule it was taken over by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in the 3rd century BCE, and it was destroyed in the wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>. It lay on the border that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exarchate-of-ravenna">Exarchate of Ravenna</a> shared with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, and it was presented to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a> by its Frankish conqueror in 754, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donation-of-pepin">Donation of Pepin</a>. It then passed back and forth between the popes and the archbishops of Ravenna, was briefly a communal republic from 1183 to 1198, and was long contested between popes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperors</a>.</p><p>The brief rule of the Forlì family of the Ordelaffi was crushed in 1357 by papal troops led by Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gil-alvarez-carrillo-de-albornoz">Gil de Albornoz</a>, after a long siege endured by Cia degli Ordelaffi. The town revolted again in 1377 and was recaptured by Breton troops under the condottiere <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hawkwood">John Hawkwood</a> and the command of Robert, Cardinal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, later the antipope Clement VII. Acting as legate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xi">Pope Gregory XI</a>, he directed the murder of between 2,500 and 5,000 civilians, an atrocity even by the laws of war of the time, called the Cesena Bloodbath. The following year what remained of Cesena was assigned to Galeotto I Malatesta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>From 1379 to 1465 the city recovered and prospered under the Malatesta, who rebuilt the castle, the Rocca Malatestiana. The fortress was begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gil-alvarez-carrillo-de-albornoz">Cardinal Albornoz</a> in 1380 and completed by the papal governor Lorenzo Zane in 1480. It is octagonal, with two main towers, the taller maschio and the shorter foemina, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> later used it to imprison <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caterina-sforza">Caterina Sforza</a>. Near the castle, Malatesta Novello built the Malatestiana Library, a fine example of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> library that holds many valuable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and is a UNESCO Memory of the World site.</p><p>After Novello's death in 1465 Cesena returned to the Papal States, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> seized it and made it the capital of his short-lived duchy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vi">Pope Pius VI</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vii">Pope Pius VII</a> were born in the city and Pope Pius VIII was its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, which earned it the title of city of the three popes. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> it lost numerous monasteries and churches, and some of its citizens played notable roles in the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it lay near the Gothic Line and suffered heavily from bombing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cesena” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1501 his father named him Duke of Romagna, with <b>Cesena</b> as the seat of government.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-romagna">Duchy of Romagna</a></li><li><blockquote>They ruled, sometimes as papal vicars and sometimes in open rebellion, over Forlì and at times <b>Cesena</b> and other Romagna towns.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-forli">Lordship of Forli</a></li><li><blockquote>The Malatesta were warlords and condottieri who governed Rimini, <b>Cesena</b>, Fano and other towns.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-rimini">Lordship of Rimini</a></li></ul></section>
